      {
        "type": "heading",
        "attrs": {
          "level": 2
        },
        "content": [
          {
            "type": "text",
            "text": "Method"
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "type": "orderedList",
        "content": [
          {
            "type": "listItem",
            "content": [
              {
                "type": "paragraph",
                "content": [
                  {
                    "type": "text",
                    "text": "Heat the olive oil in a large saucepan or wide pan over medium heat. Add the onion and garlic and cook for 8 minutes, stirring occasionally, until soft and turning translucent."
                  }
                ]
              }
            ]
          },
          {
            "type": "listItem",
            "content": [
              {
                "type": "paragraph",
                "content": [
                  {
                    "type": "text",
                    "text": "Add the pepper strips and stir to coat in the oil. Cook uncovered for 5 minutes, stirring occasionally, until the peppers start to soften at the edges."
                  }
                ]
              }
            ]
          },
          {
            "type": "listItem",
            "content": [
              {
                "type": "paragraph",
                "content": [
                  {
                    "type": "text",
                    "text": "Add the chopped tomatoes and stir to combine. Season generously with black pepper. Cover the pan and reduce the heat to low."
                  }
                ]
              }
            ]
          },
          {
            "type": "listItem",
            "content": [
              {
                "type": "paragraph",
                "content": [
                  {
                    "type": "text",
                    "text": "Cook for 30 minutes, stirring every 10 minutes, until the peppers are very soft and the tomatoes have broken down into a thick sauce coating everything. If it looks too wet, remove the lid for the final 10 minutes."
                  }
                ]
              }
            ]
          },
          {
            "type": "listItem",
            "content": [
              {
                "type": "paragraph",
                "content": [
                  {
                    "type": "text",
                    "text": "Remove from the heat. Stir in the red wine vinegar and tear over the basil leaves. Taste and adjust. Serve warm or at room temperature."
                  }
                ]
              }
            ]
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "type": "troubleshooter",
        "content": [
          {
            "type": "troubleshootItem",
            "attrs": {
              "problem": "Peppers still firm after 30 minutes",
              "solution": "Different peppers cook at different rates. Keep the lid on and continue cooking, checking every 10 minutes. They should become very soft and slightly collapsed."
            }
          },
          {
            "type": "troubleshootItem",
            "attrs": {
              "problem": "Sauce is watery",
              "solution": "Remove the lid and increase the heat to medium. Stir more frequently and let the liquid evaporate until the sauce coats the peppers thickly."
            }
          },
          {
            "type": "troubleshootItem",
            "attrs": {
              "problem": "Dish tastes flat",
              "solution": "Check the seasoning - it may need more black pepper. The red wine vinegar at the end adds brightness; you can increase this slightly. Make sure enough time was given for the slow braise."
            }
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "type": "heading",
        "attrs": {
          "level": 2
        },
        "content": [
          {
            "type": "text",
            "text": "Where this dish lives"
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "type": "paragraph",
        "content": [
          {
            "type": "text",
            "text": "Peperonata is most common in southern Italy. Campania, Calabria, and Sicily all make versions of it. It is a summer dish made when red and yellow peppers are cheap and abundant. In some regions it is served cold the next day as an antipasto. In others it goes on top of bruschetta or alongside grilled meat."
          }
        ]
      }
    ]
  }
